<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tôn Thất Thuyết</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ton That Thuyet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tôn Thất Thuyết was a Vietnamese mandarin and regent of the Nguyen court who led resistance to French rule. On 5 July 1885 he launched an attack on the French garrison at Huế, and after its failure fled with the boy emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ham-nghi">Hàm Nghi</a> and issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/can-vuong">Cần Vương</a> edict calling on the people to rise. He later sought help in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, where he died in exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ton-that-thuyet.jpg" alt="Tôn Thất Thuyết" width="269" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ph%E1%BB%A5_ch%C3%ADnh_%C4%91%E1%BA%A1i_th%E1%BA%A7n_T%C3%B4n_Th%E1%BA%A5t_Thuy%E1%BA%BFt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
